it's easy enough to say i'm going to  start learning this language but i know  from experience it's a lot of work hi  there steve kaufman here today i want to  sort of ask myself a question  why do you want to learn another  language  and  but first before i get into the subject  uh if you enjoy my videos please  subscribe click on the bell for  notifications  and if you follow me on a podcast  service please leave a review  so  why has this subject suddenly come to my  mind well  my a large part of my inspiration for  learning languages comes from  different series that my wife watches on  netflix and i kind of go in and out of  the tv room and sometimes sit down and  watch  and she was watching an  indian movie  gangubai or something  uh and of course it's it's in bombay  it's 100 years ago or whenever 80 years  ago and it's very exotic and all of  these things that sort of  you know and of course  i pick out the odd word  in hindi that sounds a bit like persian  the odd one  and it then becomes very tempting to say  you know what i should learn  hindi or punjabi or one of those indian  languages  and so then i say to myself well here  you are you've been struggling with  persian and arabic and of course  neglecting other languages that you've  learned  why would you take on yet another  language  and  you know this is an interesting question  obviously the more languages you have  or the more languages you try to learn  the less well you're going to speak in  languages that you have already spent  time trying to learn  you know  i sometimes say to myself why did you  ever bother trying to learn  persian and arabic when you know i maybe  you should  focus on korean which is not at a level  that you would like  and the the simple answer is  that i'm always curious  you know as imperfect as my arabic and  persian are and i will may perhaps do a  uh  maybe a conversation with my tutor again  in those languages and you'll see that  it's full of holes and yet  it's at a level where i enjoy them  uh i enjoy listening to my um you know  radio fardo  podcast news in farsi i understand a  fair amount of it  i enjoy for example my jordanian  cartoon series so i'm enjoying  participating to some extent in that  language in that culture however  imperfectly  so  whatever i put in  i feel i get back  now  how exciting would it be to have some  sense  of how  some indian language works  probably hindi because it's the most  widely spoken and of course the  bollywood movies are in hindi  but here in vancouver most of the people  from india are in fact from the punjab  so they speak punjabi  probably i have no idea how mutually  intelligible those languages are but  it would be fun  to do them and so it becomes fun  it's it's a challenge like part of  learning other languages simply because  the language is there  so it's like why do you climb a mountain  because the mountain is there  you know i look back to when i wrote my  book the linguist on language  i guess i was 57 or so  and i had nine languages and i had  enjoyed every one of those nine  languages well now i'm  approaching my 20th language  mostly since the age of 60  and it's brought me so much and i can  think of the  the egyptian movies that i watch trying  very hard to understand something not  understanding too much but  but because of my exposure to that  language and the few things that i do  understand i'm more involved i i can  feel and smell and touch the culture  more  uh similarly with  persian where  sahara in iran has sent me some  iranian movies and i'm confident maybe  more confident in persian than in arabic  that i'll eventually be able to  understand those movies quite well  uh so it brings me all of that and and i  think i've made the point in a number of  my other videos that whatever i achieve  is good it's all a plus there is no  negative  perhaps the only negative is that i  haven't spent that time working on some  of my other languages  but i can go back to them whatever i put  into them is there i'm not going to lose  it it might take me a little while to  refresh them  so  i don't know why learn another language  i guess the question can be asked of  someone like me who has tried to learn  say 20 languages  and who might look at trying to  learn about another yet another language  or two or three  or someone who is still struggling with  their first foreign language and of  course for many people it's not a hobby  for many people it's it's there's a  practical need  they might be an immigrant or a refugee  in a country in germany or in north  america or somewhere they have to learn  the local language  or  if you're living somewhere where you do  business with people from another  country and you need that language then  of course there's a greater sort of need  for the language  but i'm not in that situation i'm in the  situation where i  learn languages for pleasure  and uh why would i take on yet another  language with all of and of course it's  easy enough to say i'm going to start  learning this language but i know from  experience it's a lot of work so once i  commit myself to sort of  spending time with that language  it's going to be another  sort of time sump  but  it's an enjoyable pursuit for me  and not only is the actual study of the  language enjoyable but everything that  comes with it the discovery of the  culture getting closer to those people  and seeing those people less sort of in  a caricature form or stereotype form but  rather seeing them as they are and  hearing how they speak and how they  express themselves so that's why i  continue to add languages although i  don't achieve a high level of fluency  i do achieve a very worthwhile level of  familiarity with those languages and and  that's why i would continue learning  languages and i would like to  suggest you have a look at two languages  or two videos that i did on the subject  uh one was six months ago where i talk  about learning two languages at once  and another one three months ago where i  talk about learning multiple languages  at the same time  and that might give you a little  different perspective on the issue of  taking on  uh different languages or continuing to  challenge yourself with more languages  when you already know some and and i'm  sure i'm not the only one in this  situation so i look forward to hearing  from you  bye for now

